From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 00:26:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: Add the Acer Aspire 14 AI and 16 AI

Add device trees for the Acer Aspire 14 AI (A14-11M) and Aspire 16 AI
(A16-11M), on the Snapdragon X X1-26-100 (Purwa). The two laptops' ACPI
tables list the same I2C, GPIO and interrupt resources, so they share
one dtsi, and each .dts sets the model, compatible and firmware paths.
The tree describes:

- the keyboard and touchpad (HID over I2C), Acer's HID device, the lid
  switch, and the touchscreen of the -11MT models, disabled
- the eDP panel on DP3 through the generic panel driver, and its PWM
  backlight
- both USB-C ports through pmic-glink, with PS8830 retimers
- both USB-A ports on the multiport controller, with their PTN3222
  repeaters
- HDMI on the third QMP PHY in DP mode
- the NVMe SSD on PCIe 6a
- the WCN7850 Wi-Fi and Bluetooth module on PCIe 4 and UART14
- the ADSP, CDSP and GPU, with firmware under
  qcom/x1p42100/ACER/<model>/

The layout follows the ASUS Zenbook A14, whose ACPI tables list the same
USB-C, USB, GPU and HDMI resources on the same chip. The Wi-Fi and
Bluetooth nodes follow the Lenovo ThinkBook 16. ACPI doesn't name the
HDMI bridge, so the Zenbook's parade,ps185hdm is an assumption.

Audio and the cameras are left out. There is no audio topology for
these laptops, and the speaker amplifiers need Acer's tuning.

The Aspire 16 AI booted this tree from Ubuntu's live USB on a 7.0
kernel, with the panel, keyboard, NVMe, all four USB ports, battery and
Bluetooth working. Wi-Fi stops at board data, which linux-firmware
doesn't have for the module's subsystem ID 1a3b:6580. The Aspire 14 AI
hasn't been tried.

Signed-off-by: Birk Skyum <birk.skyum@pm.me>
